IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ea-core`

-- Employment Act 1968 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- "version in force from 5/12/2025" -- LAYER 1, PART 10, SECTIONS 89 AND 90 (sick leave; the offence).  Part ea2.
-- Source text: inputs/EmA1968.txt (a converter rendering, not the official text).
--
-- Notes for the reader.
--   * Seam S6 (the Third Schedule's daily rate) is taken as the input `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day`.
--   * The keel's "medical officer" is taken as the input `the answer of s 2(1) "medical officer"`.
--   * A plain BOOLEAN or NUMBER answer is a date or count comparison and carries no legal "no".
--   * Months of service are COMPLETED months, so "at least 3 months but less than 4 months" is 3 completed months.
--   * Section 89(9) (the terms and revocation of the Commissioner's order) is machinery; it has no function here.

§ `Part 10 -- how many days of paid sick leave (s 89(1) to (3))`

-- s 89(1): "Any employee who has served an employer for a period of not less than 6 months is entitled,
-- after examination by a medical practitioner, to such paid sick leave, as may be certified by the
-- medical practitioner, not exceeding in the aggregate (a) if no hospitalisation is necessary, 14 days
-- in each year; or (b) if hospitalisation is necessary, the lesser of (i) 60 days in each year; (ii) the
-- aggregate of 14 days plus the number of days on which the employee is hospitalised."
-- src:3583 | 89.—(1) Any employee who has served an employer for a period of
-- src:3584 | not less than 6 months is entitled, after examination by a medical
-- src:3585 | practitioner, to such paid sick leave, as may be certified by the
-- src:3586 | medical practitioner, not exceeding in the aggregate —
-- src:3588 | (a) if no hospitalisation is necessary, 14 days in each year; or
-- src:3590 | (b) if hospitalisation is necessary, the lesser of the following:
-- src:3592 | (i) 60 days in each year;
-- src:3594 | (ii) the aggregate of 14 days plus the number of days on
-- src:3595 | which the employee is hospitalised.
@ref s 89(1) (src:3583-3595)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
      `hospitalisation is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days on which the employee is hospitalised` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 89(1) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 6
    THEN
        IF `hospitalisation is necessary`
        THEN JUST (min 60 (14 PLUS `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`))
        ELSE JUST 14
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) is for an employee who has served an employer for not less than 6 months; subsection (2) is for 3 to 6 months
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 89(2)(a): at least 3 months but less than 4: 5 days; with hospitalisation the lesser of 15 and 5 plus the days hospitalised.
-- src:3604 | (a) where the employee has served the employer for a period
-- src:3605 | of at least 3 months but less than 4 months —
-- src:3607 | (i) if no hospitalisation is necessary, 5 days in each year;
-- src:3608 | or
-- src:3610 | (ii) if hospitalisation is necessary, the lesser of the
-- src:3611 | following:
-- src:3613 | (A) 15 days in each year;
-- src:3615 | (B) the aggregate of 5 days plus the number of days
-- src:3616 | on which the employee is hospitalised;
@ref s 89(2)(a) (src:3604-3616)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
      `hospitalisation is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days on which the employee is hospitalised` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 89(2)(a) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 3 AND `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 4
    THEN
        IF `hospitalisation is necessary`
        THEN JUST (min 15 (5 PLUS `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`))
        ELSE JUST 5
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (a) is for an employee who has served the employer for at least 3 months but less than 4 months
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 89(2)(b): at least 4 months but less than 5: 8 days; with hospitalisation the lesser of 30 and 8 plus the days hospitalised.
-- src:3618 | (b) where the employee has served the employer for a period
-- src:3619 | of at least 4 months but less than 5 months —
-- src:3621 | (i) if no hospitalisation is necessary, 8 days in each year;
-- src:3622 | or
-- src:3627 | (ii) if hospitalisation is necessary, the lesser of the
-- src:3628 | following:
-- src:3630 | (A) 30 days in each year;
-- src:3632 | (B) the aggregate of 8 days plus the number of days
-- src:3633 | on which the employee is hospitalised; or
@ref s 89(2)(b) (src:3618-3633)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
      `hospitalisation is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days on which the employee is hospitalised` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 89(2)(b) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 4 AND `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 5
    THEN
        IF `hospitalisation is necessary`
        THEN JUST (min 30 (8 PLUS `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`))
        ELSE JUST 8
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (b) is for an employee who has served the employer for at least 4 months but less than 5 months
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 89(2)(c): at least 5 months but less than 6: 11 days; with hospitalisation the lesser of 45 and 11 plus the days hospitalised.
-- src:3635 | (c) where the employee has served the employer for a period
-- src:3636 | of at least 5 months but less than 6 months —
-- src:3638 | (i) if no hospitalisation is necessary, 11 days in each
-- src:3639 | year; or
-- src:3641 | (ii) if hospitalisation is necessary, the lesser of the
-- src:3642 | following:
-- src:3644 | (A) 45 days in each year;
-- src:3646 | (B) the aggregate of 11 days plus the number of
-- src:3647 | days on which the employee is hospitalised.
@ref s 89(2)(c) (src:3635-3647)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
      `hospitalisation is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days on which the employee is hospitalised` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 89(2)(c) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 5 AND `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 6
    THEN
        IF `hospitalisation is necessary`
        THEN JUST (min 45 (11 PLUS `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`))
        ELSE JUST 11
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (c) is for an employee who has served the employer for at least 5 months but less than 6 months
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The yes/no twins of the three tier rules above (fork F18).  Each tier opens "where the employee has served the employer for a
-- period of at least N months but less than M months", and a "where" that is not met is the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05).
-- The tier rules above answer a number of days and cannot say no, so these say it: is this the paragraph that applies?
-- src:3604 | (a) where the employee has served the employer for a period
-- src:3605 | of at least 3 months but less than 4 months —
@ref s 89(2)(a) (src:3604-3605)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(2)(a) this paragraph applies to the employee` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 3 AND `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 4
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where the employee has served the employer for a period of at least 3 months but less than 4 months"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3618 | (b) where the employee has served the employer for a period
-- src:3619 | of at least 4 months but less than 5 months —
@ref s 89(2)(b) (src:3618-3619)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(2)(b) this paragraph applies to the employee` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 4 AND `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 5
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where the employee has served the employer for a period of at least 4 months but less than 5 months"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:3635 | (c) where the employee has served the employer for a period
-- src:3636 | of at least 5 months but less than 6 months —
@ref s 89(2)(c) (src:3635-3636)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(2)(c) this paragraph applies to the employee` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 5 AND `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 6
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where the employee has served the employer for a period of at least 5 months but less than 6 months"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 89(2): "Any employee who has served an employer for a period of at least 3 months but less than 6
-- months is entitled, after examination by a medical practitioner, to such paid sick leave, as may be
-- certified by the medical practitioner, not exceeding in the aggregate" the days of paragraphs (a) to (c).
-- src:3599 | (2) Any employee who has served an employer for a period of at
-- src:3600 | least 3 months but less than 6 months is entitled, after examination by
-- src:3601 | a medical practitioner, to such paid sick leave, as may be certified by
-- src:3602 | the medical practitioner, not exceeding in the aggregate —
@ref s 89(2) (src:3599-3602)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
      `hospitalisation is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days on which the employee is hospitalised` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 89(2) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 3
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) is for an employee who has served an employer for at least 3 months
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 4
        THEN `s 89(2)(a) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` `the completed months the employee has served the employer` `hospitalisation is necessary` `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`
        ELSE
            IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 5
            THEN `s 89(2)(b) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` `the completed months the employee has served the employer` `hospitalisation is necessary` `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`
            ELSE `s 89(2)(c) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` `the completed months the employee has served the employer` `hospitalisation is necessary` `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`

-- s 89(1) and (2) together: the days of paid sick leave the employee is entitled to on a certificate, after
-- examination by a medical practitioner: the days certified, within what remains of the aggregate for the year.
-- src:3583 | 89.—(1) Any employee who has served an employer for a period of
-- src:3584 | not less than 6 months is entitled, after examination by a medical
-- src:3585 | practitioner, to such paid sick leave, as may be certified by the
-- src:3586 | medical practitioner, not exceeding in the aggregate —
@ref s 89(1) (src:3583-3586)
-- src:3599 | (2) Any employee who has served an employer for a period of at
-- src:3600 | least 3 months but less than 6 months is entitled, after examination by
-- src:3601 | a medical practitioner, to such paid sick leave, as may be certified by
-- src:3602 | the medical practitioner, not exceeding in the aggregate —
@ref s 89(2) (src:3599-3602)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
      `the employee was examined by a medical practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days of sick leave certified by the medical practitioner` IS A NUMBER
      `hospitalisation is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days on which the employee is hospitalised` IS A NUMBER
      `the days of paid sick leave already taken in the year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 89 the days of paid sick leave the employee is entitled to on the certificate` MEANS
    IF NOT `the employee was examined by a medical practitioner`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the entitlement is "after examination by a medical practitioner"
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        CONSIDER (IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` AT LEAST 6
                  THEN `s 89(1) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` `the completed months the employee has served the employer` `hospitalisation is necessary` `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`
                  ELSE `s 89(2) the most days of paid sick leave in the year` `the completed months the employee has served the employer` `hospitalisation is necessary` `the days on which the employee is hospitalised`)
        WHEN JUST most THEN JUST (min `the days of sick leave certified by the medical practitioner` (max 0 (most MINUS `the days of paid sick leave already taken in the year`)))
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the entitlement begins after 3 months of service
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 89(3)(a): "an employee is hospitalised if the employee is warded in a hospital in such circumstances
-- as may be prescribed or is under quarantine (whether or not in a hospital) under any written law".
-- src:3651 | (3) For the purposes of this section —
-- src:3653 | (a) an employee is hospitalised if the employee is warded in a
-- src:3654 | hospital in such circumstances as may be prescribed or is
-- src:3655 | under quarantine (whether or not in a hospital) under any
-- src:3656 | written law; and
@ref s 89(3)(a) (src:3651-3656)
GIVEN `the employee is warded in a hospital in circumstances prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee is under quarantine under a written law, in a hospital or not` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(3)(a) the employee is hospitalised` MEANS
    IF `the employee is warded in a hospital in circumstances prescribed` OR `the employee is under quarantine under a written law, in a hospital or not`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an employee is hospitalised if the employee is warded in a hospital"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 89(3)(b): treated as hospitalised "for any period the employee is not an in-patient of a hospital, or
-- stops being an in-patient of a hospital after being warded in that hospital in accordance with
-- paragraph (a), if the employee is certified, by a medical practitioner employed by a hospital approved
-- by the Minister (i) to be ill enough to require hospitalisation during that period; or (ii) to need rest
-- or further medical treatment during that period in order to recover after the employee's discharge".
-- src:3658 | (b) an employee is to be treated as hospitalised for any period
-- src:3659 | the employee is not an in-patient of a hospital, or stops
-- src:3660 | being an in-patient of a hospital after being warded in that
-- src:3661 | hospital in accordance with paragraph (a), if the employee
-- src:3662 | is certified, by a medical practitioner employed by a
-- src:3663 | hospital approved by the Minister —
-- src:3665 | (i) to be ill enough to require hospitalisation during that
-- src:3666 | period; or
-- src:3668 | (ii) to need rest or further medical treatment during that
-- src:3669 | period in order to recover after the employee’s
-- src:3670 | discharge from a hospital.
@ref s 89(3)(b) (src:3658-3670)
GIVEN `the period is one in which the employee is not an in-patient, or stops being an in-patient after being warded in accordance with paragraph (a)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a medical practitioner employed by a hospital approved by the Minister certified the employee to be ill enough to require hospitalisation during that period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a medical practitioner employed by a hospital approved by the Minister certified the employee to need rest or further medical treatment during that period in order to recover after discharge` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(3)(b) the employee is to be treated as hospitalised for the period` MEANS
    IF `the period is one in which the employee is not an in-patient, or stops being an in-patient after being warded in accordance with paragraph (a)`
       AND (`a medical practitioner employed by a hospital approved by the Minister certified the employee to be ill enough to require hospitalisation during that period`
            OR `a medical practitioner employed by a hospital approved by the Minister certified the employee to need rest or further medical treatment during that period in order to recover after discharge`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the employee is certified, by a medical practitioner employed by a hospital approved"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 89(3): the two limbs together: hospitalised (a) or treated as hospitalised (b).  Where both say no the
-- employee is not hospitalised; where neither is known the answer is unknown.
-- src:3651 | (3) For the purposes of this section —
-- src:3653 | (a) an employee is hospitalised if the employee is warded in a
-- src:3654 | hospital in such circumstances as may be prescribed or is
-- src:3655 | under quarantine (whether or not in a hospital) under any
-- src:3656 | written law; and
-- src:3658 | (b) an employee is to be treated as hospitalised for any period
-- src:3659 | the employee is not an in-patient of a hospital, or stops
-- src:3660 | being an in-patient of a hospital after being warded in that
-- src:3661 | hospital in accordance with paragraph (a), if the employee
-- src:3662 | is certified, by a medical practitioner employed by a
-- src:3663 | hospital approved by the Minister —
-- src:3665 | (i) to be ill enough to require hospitalisation during that
-- src:3666 | period; or
-- src:3668 | (ii) to need rest or further medical treatment during that
-- src:3669 | period in order to recover after the employee’s
-- src:3670 | discharge from a hospital.
@ref s 89(3) (src:3651-3670)
GIVEN `the answer of s 89(3)(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 89(3)(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(3) the employee is hospitalised or treated as hospitalised` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the answer of s 89(3)(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the answer of s 89(3)(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an employee is hospitalised if the employee is warded in a hospital"
        IF `the answer of s 89(3)(a)` EQUALS JUST FALSE AND `the answer of s 89(3)(b)` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): one of the two limbs is not answered
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `Part 10 -- certificates, the pay for sick leave, and the days on which it is not paid (s 89(4) to (7))`

-- s 89(4): sick leave "(a) which is not certified by a medical practitioner; or (b) which is certified by
-- a medical practitioner not appointed by the employer, but of which the employee did not inform or attempt
-- to inform the employer within 48 hours after its commencement, is deemed to have absented himself or
-- herself from work without the employer's permission and without reasonable excuse".
-- src:3677 | (4) An employee who absents himself or herself on sick leave —
-- src:3679 | (a) which is not certified by a medical practitioner; or
-- src:3681 | (b) which is certified by a medical practitioner not appointed
-- src:3682 | by the employer, but of which the employee did not inform
-- src:3683 | or attempt to inform the employer within 48 hours after its
-- src:3684 | commencement,
-- src:3686 | is deemed to have absented himself or herself from work without the
-- src:3687 | employer’s permission and without reasonable excuse for the days on
-- src:3688 | which the employee is so absent from work.
@ref s 89(4) (src:3677-3688)
GIVEN `the sick leave was certified by a medical practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the medical practitioner was appointed by the employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee informed or attempted to inform the employer within 48 hours after the commencement of the sick leave` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(4) the employee is deemed to have absented himself or herself without the employer's permission and without reasonable excuse` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the sick leave was certified by a medical practitioner`)
       OR ((NOT `the medical practitioner was appointed by the employer`)
           AND (NOT `the employee informed or attempted to inform the employer within 48 hours after the commencement of the sick leave`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the deeming is for sick leave not certified by a medical practitioner, or certified by one not appointed by the employer of which the employer was not told in time
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 89(5): "The employer must pay the employee for every day of such sick leave (a) where no
-- hospitalisation is necessary, at the gross rate of pay excluding any allowance payable in respect of
-- shift work; and (b) where hospitalisation is necessary, at the gross rate of pay."
-- src:3692 | (5) The employer must pay the employee for every day of such sick
-- src:3693 | leave —
-- src:3695 | (a) where no hospitalisation is necessary, at the gross rate of
-- src:3696 | pay excluding any allowance payable in respect of shift
-- src:3697 | work; and
-- src:3699 | (b) where hospitalisation is necessary, at the gross rate of pay.
@ref s 89(5) (src:3692-3699)
GIVEN `the days of paid sick leave` IS A NUMBER
      `hospitalisation is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the allowance payable in respect of shift work for a day, included in that rate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 89(5) the amount the employer must pay for the days of sick leave` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day`
    WHEN JUST rate THEN
        IF `hospitalisation is necessary`
        THEN JUST (`the days of paid sick leave` TIMES rate)
        ELSE JUST (`the days of paid sick leave` TIMES (rate MINUS `the allowance payable in respect of shift work for a day, included in that rate`))
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the gross rate of pay for a day is worked out under the Third Schedule, which is not given
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 89(6): "Despite subsection (5), an employee is not entitled to paid sick leave on a rest day or on a
-- holiday to which he or she is entitled under section 36 or 88 respectively or on any day of paid annual
-- leave or on a day when he or she is not required to work under his or her contract of service or on a
-- day when he or she is on leave of absence without pay granted by the employer at the employee's request."
-- A day substituted for a public holiday is not named; the Act does not say it is a holiday under s 88 (fork F12).
-- src:3701 | (6) Despite subsection (5), an employee is not entitled to paid sick
-- src:3702 | leave on a rest day or on a holiday to which he or she is entitled under
-- src:3703 | section 36 or 88 respectively or on any day of paid annual leave or on
-- src:3704 | a day when he or she is not required to work under his or her contract
-- src:3705 | of service or on a day when he or she is on leave of absence without
-- src:3706 | pay granted by the employer at the employee’s request.
@ref s 89(6) (src:3701-3706)
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of day`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(6) the employee is entitled to paid sick leave on the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an employee is not entitled to paid sick leave on a rest day"
    WHEN `Day: a rest day` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "or on a holiday to which he or she is entitled under section 36 or 88 respectively"
    WHEN `Day: a public holiday` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "or on any day of paid annual leave"
    WHEN `Day: a day of paid annual leave` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "or on a day when he or she is not required to work under his or her contract of service"
    WHEN `Day: a day the employee is not required to work` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "or on a day when he or she is on leave of absence without pay granted by the employer at the employee's request"
    WHEN `Day: a day of no-pay leave` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection names rest days, holidays, paid annual leave, days not required to work and no-pay leave only
    WHEN `Day: a working day` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the Act does not say that a day substituted for a public holiday is a holiday to which the employee is entitled under section 88
    WHEN `Day: a day substituted for a public holiday` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection names rest days, holidays, paid annual leave, days not required to work and no-pay leave only
    WHEN `Day: a day of paid sick leave` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection names rest days, holidays, paid annual leave, days not required to work and no-pay leave only
    WHEN `Day: a day of childcare leave` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection names rest days, holidays, paid annual leave, days not required to work and no-pay leave only
    WHEN `Day: a day within a maternity benefit period` THEN NOTHING

-- s 89(7): "An employee is not entitled to paid sick leave for the period during which he or she is receiving
-- or is entitled to receive compensation for temporary incapacity under paragraph 4 of the First Schedule
-- to the Work Injury Compensation Act 2019 or paragraph 4 of the Third Schedule to the Work Injury
-- Compensation Act (Cap. 354, 2009 Revised Edition) repealed by that Act."
-- src:3708 | (7) An employee is not entitled to paid sick leave for the period
-- src:3709 | during which he or she is receiving or is entitled to receive
-- src:3710 | compensation for temporary incapacity under paragraph 4 of the
-- src:3711 | First Schedule to the Work Injury Compensation Act 2019 or
-- src:3712 | paragraph 4 of the Third Schedule to the Work Injury Compensation
-- src:3713 | Act (Cap. 354, 2009 Revised Edition) repealed by that Act.
@ref s 89(7) (src:3708-3713)
GIVEN `the employee is receiving or is entitled to receive compensation for temporary incapacity under paragraph 4 of the First Schedule to the Work Injury Compensation Act 2019, or under the corresponding paragraph of the Act it replaced` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(7) the employee is entitled to paid sick leave for the period` MEANS
    IF `the employee is receiving or is entitled to receive compensation for temporary incapacity under paragraph 4 of the First Schedule to the Work Injury Compensation Act 2019, or under the corresponding paragraph of the Act it replaced`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An employee is not entitled to paid sick leave for the period during which"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection takes away paid sick leave only for a period of compensation for temporary incapacity under the Work Injury Compensation Act
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 10 -- the cost of the examination, cosmetic treatment, and the offence (s 89(7A) to (10), s 90)`

-- s 89(7A): "Where an employee has served an employer for a period of at least 3 months, the employer is
-- liable to bear, or to reimburse the employee, the fees of an examination of the employee by a medical
-- practitioner, if (a) the medical practitioner is appointed by the employer or is a medical officer; and
-- (b) after the examination, the employee is certified by the medical practitioner to be entitled to paid sick leave."
-- src:3717 | (7A) Where an employee has served an employer for a period of at
-- src:3718 | least 3 months, the employer is liable to bear, or to reimburse the
-- src:3719 | employee, the fees of an examination of the employee by a medical
-- src:3720 | practitioner, if —
-- src:3722 | (a) the medical practitioner is appointed by the employer or is
-- src:3723 | a medical officer; and
-- src:3728 | (b) after the examination, the employee is certified by the
-- src:3729 | medical practitioner to be entitled to paid sick leave.
@ref s 89(7A) (src:3717-3729)
GIVEN `the completed months the employee has served the employer` IS A NUMBER
      `the medical practitioner is appointed by the employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 2(1) "medical officer"` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the employee is certified by the medical practitioner to be entitled to paid sick leave` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(7A) the employer is liable to bear or reimburse the fees of the examination` MEANS
    IF `the completed months the employee has served the employer` LESS THAN 3
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an employee has served an employer for a period of at least 3 months, the employer is liable to bear"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE
        IF NOT `the employee is certified by the medical practitioner to be entitled to paid sick leave`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the medical practitioner is appointed by the employer or is a medical officer; and"
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE
            IF `the medical practitioner is appointed by the employer` OR `the answer of s 2(1) "medical officer"` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE
                CONSIDER `the answer of s 2(1) "medical officer"`
                -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the medical practitioner is appointed by the employer or is a medical officer; and"
                WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
                -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the medical practitioner is a medical officer is the answer of s 2(1) "medical officer", which the Act did not give on these facts
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
                -- NOTHING-kind (silent): this case is covered by the branch above
                WHEN JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING

-- s 89(8): "An employer is deemed to fulfil the employer's obligation under subsection (7A) if (a) the
-- Commissioner ... by written order directs that the employer has fulfilled that obligation for so long
-- as the employer provides such a healthcare scheme ...; or (b) the employer complies with such other
-- requirement as the Minister may, by regulations, prescribe."
-- src:3733 | (8) An employer is deemed to fulfil the employer’s obligation under
-- src:3734 | subsection (7A) if —
-- src:3736 | (a) the Commissioner, after considering the merits of any
-- src:3737 | healthcare scheme that the employer provides to the
-- src:3738 | employer’s employees and such other matters as the
-- src:3739 | Commissioner may consider relevant, by written order
-- src:3740 | directs that the employer has fulfilled that obligation for so
-- src:3741 | long as the employer provides such a healthcare scheme for
-- src:3742 | the employer’s employees; or
-- src:3744 | (b) the employer complies with such other requirement as the
-- src:3745 | Minister may, by regulations, prescribe.
@ref s 89(8) (src:3733-3745)
GIVEN `the Commissioner has by written order directed that the employer has fulfilled the obligation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer provides the healthcare scheme for its employees at the time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer complies with the other requirement the Minister has prescribed by regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(8) the employer is deemed to fulfil its obligation under subsection (7A)` MEANS
    IF (`the Commissioner has by written order directed that the employer has fulfilled the obligation` AND `the employer provides the healthcare scheme for its employees at the time`)
       OR `the employer complies with the other requirement the Minister has prescribed by regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An employer is deemed to fulfil the employer's obligation under subsection (7A) if"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 89(10): "This section does not apply to any employee seeking or undergoing medical treatment which, in the
-- opinion of a medical practitioner performing the examination under subsection (1) or (2), is for cosmetic purposes."
-- src:3760 | (10) This section does not apply to any employee seeking or
-- src:3761 | undergoing medical treatment which, in the opinion of a medical
-- src:3762 | practitioner performing the examination under subsection (1) or (2),
-- src:3763 | is for cosmetic purposes.
@ref s 89(10) (src:3760-3763)
GIVEN `in the opinion of the medical practitioner performing the examination the treatment is for cosmetic purposes` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 89(10) section 89 applies to the employee` MEANS
    IF `in the opinion of the medical practitioner performing the examination the treatment is for cosmetic purposes`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This section does not apply to any employee seeking or undergoing medical treatment which"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection takes the section away only from an employee seeking or undergoing treatment that the examining practitioner considers cosmetic
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 90(1): "Any employer who employs any person as an employee contrary to the provisions of this Part or fails
-- to pay any salary in accordance with the provisions of this Part shall be guilty of an offence".
-- src:3769 | 90.—(1) Any employer who employs any person as an employee
-- src:3770 | contrary to the provisions of this Part or fails to pay any salary in
-- src:3771 | accordance with the provisions of this Part shall be guilty of an
-- src:3772 | offence and shall be liable on conviction to a fine not exceeding
-- src:3773 | $5,000, and for a second or subsequent offence to a fine not
-- src:3778 | exceeding $10,000 or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding
-- src:3779 | 12 months or to both.
@ref s 90(1) (src:3769-3779)
GIVEN `the employer employs a person as an employee contrary to the provisions of this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer fails to pay a salary in accordance with the provisions of this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 90(1) the employer is guilty of an offence` MEANS
    IF `the employer employs a person as an employee contrary to the provisions of this Part` OR `the employer fails to pay a salary in accordance with the provisions of this Part`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the offence is employing a person contrary to this Part or failing to pay a salary in accordance with this Part
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 90(1): "a fine not exceeding $5,000, and for a second or subsequent offence to a fine not exceeding
-- $10,000 or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 12 months or to both".  For a first offence the text
-- gives a fine only, so no imprisonment is available (0 months; see NEEDS-CORE.md).
@ref s 90(1) (src:3769-3779)
GIVEN `it is a second or subsequent offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Penalty on conviction`
`s 90(1) the penalty on conviction` MEANS
    IF `it is a second or subsequent offence`
    THEN `Penalty on conviction` WITH
            `least fine in dollars` IS 0
            `greatest fine in dollars` IS JUST 10000
            `longest imprisonment in months` IS JUST 12
    ELSE `Penalty on conviction` WITH
            `least fine in dollars` IS 0
            `greatest fine in dollars` IS JUST 5000
            `longest imprisonment in months` IS JUST 0

-- s 90(2)(a): "an employer and the employer's employees or a trade union representing the employer's
-- employees may negotiate for and agree to terms of service more favourable than those contained in
-- sections 88A and 89".
-- src:3783 | (2) Despite subsection (1) —
-- src:3785 | (a) an employer and the employer’s employees or a trade
-- src:3786 | union representing the employer’s employees may
-- src:3787 | negotiate for and agree to terms of service more
-- src:3788 | favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89;
-- src:3789 | and
@ref s 90(2)(a) (src:3783-3789)
GIVEN `the terms of service are more favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 90(2)(a) the employer and the employees or their trade union may negotiate for and agree to the terms` MEANS
    IF `the terms of service are more favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph permits terms "more favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89" and says nothing of any other terms
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 90(2)(b): "it is not an offence for an employer to grant to the employer's employees terms of service
-- more favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89."
-- src:3791 | (b) it is not an offence for an employer to grant to the
-- src:3792 | employer’s employees terms of service more favourable
-- src:3793 | than those contained in sections 88A and 89.
@ref s 90(2)(b) (src:3791-3793)
GIVEN `the terms of service are more favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 90(2)(b) it is an offence for the employer to grant the terms` MEANS
    IF `the terms of service are more favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "it is not an offence for an employer to grant to the employer's employees terms of service"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph speaks only of terms more favourable than those contained in sections 88A and 89
    ELSE NOTHING
